WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.538
Picture the price board above a shop's counter.

2
00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:11.759
Ten customers can read it at once, and nobody takes turns, because nobody writes on it while they read.

3
00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:16.369
When prices change, the owner doesn't erase numbers while people read.

4
00:00:16.369 --> 00:00:19.639
She prints a new board, and hangs it up in one move.

5
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:22.130
The shop's program does the opposite.

6
00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:30.451
It keeps the prices in one map, and a refresher thread updates it in place, one price at a time, while the threads at the tills read it.

7
00:00:30.451 --> 00:00:35.652
So for a moment, a till can see the new price of bread next to the old price of milk.

8
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:43.275
In the vault lesson, even a reader needed the key, because through the glass walls it could see money in flight.

9
00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:45.707
So here's the question we're shy to ask.

10
00:00:45.707 --> 00:00:47.105
Is a lock the only way?

11
00:00:47.105 --> 00:00:51.725
Could ten threads ever read with no key at all, like customers at the board?

12
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:54.830
Look back at the races in this track.

13
00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:57.623
Eight threads adding deposits to one balance.

14
00:00:57.623 --> 00:01:01.037
Ana and Ben's transfers, locking the same two accounts.

15
00:01:01.037 --> 00:01:05.381
Each time, two ingredients: the state was shared, and it was changing.

16
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.085
Put that in a table: shared or not, changing or not.

17
00:01:09.085 --> 00:01:12.637
A race lives in one corner only, shared and changing.

18
00:01:12.637 --> 00:01:16.457
Take away either ingredient, and there's nothing to lock.

19
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.093
Let's take away change first.

20
00:01:19.093 --> 00:01:26.335
In the lesson's demo, a refresher moves four prices, bread, milk, eggs and apples, to a new version, over and over.

21
00:01:26.335 --> 00:01:30.365
For one second, the main thread reads all four, again and again.

22
00:01:30.365 --> 00:01:33.955
A read that sees two versions caught a refresh half done.

23
00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:39.127
The map is a ConcurrentHashMap, so every single put and get is thread-safe.

24
00:01:39.127 --> 00:01:40.516
How many mixed reads?

25
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.646
About 4.7 million reads, and 981,129 of them saw two versions.

26
00:01:48.646 --> 00:01:53.045
One read in five saw a price list that never existed.

27
00:01:53.045 --> 00:01:57.941
Every call was thread-safe, and the answer was still wrong.

28
00:01:58.767 --> 00:02:00.442
Now do what the owner does.

29
00:02:00.442 --> 00:02:08.012
Build the new map off to the side, where no till can see it, and make it immutable: nobody can change it after it's built.

30
00:02:08.012 --> 00:02:13.720
Then hang it up with one write to a volatile field, a field every thread always reads fresh.

31
00:02:13.933 --> 00:02:19.564
A till that reads the field gets the old board or the new one, never half of each.

32
00:02:19.564 --> 00:02:22.379
Same demo, same second: zero mixed reads.

33
00:02:22.379 --> 00:02:23.959
And nobody took a lock.

34
00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:25.396
One catch.

35
00:02:25.396 --> 00:02:30.239
A record, Java's short way to write a class of final fields, looks immutable.

36
00:02:30.239 --> 00:02:32.944
But final only bolts the frame to the wall.

37
00:02:32.944 --> 00:02:37.913
If the record holds the caller's list, the caller can still write on the paper.

38
00:02:38.133 --> 00:02:42.998
In the lesson, a quote for bread keeps the caller's list of price tiers.

39
00:02:42.998 --> 00:02:46.242
Then the caller adds a line: 10 or more at 0.01.

40
00:02:46.242 --> 00:02:49.823
Our immutable quote now sells bread at a cent a loaf.

41
00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:51.624
So print your own copy.

42
00:02:51.624 --> 00:02:57.364
Copy lists on the way in, with List.copyOf, and clone arrays on the way in and out.

43
00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:00.355
Now the other ingredient: sharing.

44
00:03:00.355 --> 00:03:03.894
Changing state is fine if only one thread can reach it.

45
00:03:03.894 --> 00:03:05.825
Remember the clerks' notepads?

46
00:03:05.825 --> 00:03:10.780
A local variable lives on one thread's stack, and no other thread can see it.

47
00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.949
When work moves to another thread, ownership moves with it.

48
00:03:14.949 --> 00:03:20.268
In the kitchen, a waiter writes a ticket, clips it to the rail, and never touches it again.

49
00:03:20.268 --> 00:03:22.080
From then on, the cook owns it.

50
00:03:22.300 --> 00:03:24.963
Now picture a waiter who saves paper.

51
00:03:24.963 --> 00:03:30.576
He erases a ticket the cook is still reading, and writes the next order on it.

52
00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:35.901
The lesson's demo did exactly that: of 5,000 tickets, 447 arrived damaged.

53
00:03:35.901 --> 00:03:38.348
With a new ticket each time, none.

54
00:03:38.567 --> 00:03:41.767
So once a ticket is on the rail, it isn't yours.

55
00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:43.167
Never touch it again.

56
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.751
One more tool gives each thread its own copy of a variable: ThreadLocal.

57
00:03:48.751 --> 00:03:54.360
But a pool thread, one thread reused for request after request, is like a hotel room.

58
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.121
The next guest finds whatever the last guest left behind.

59
00:03:58.333 --> 00:04:00.690
Six requests ran on one pool thread.

60
00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:05.468
The first request with no user ran as alice, and the next two ran as bob.

61
00:04:05.468 --> 00:04:08.807
That's one customer seeing another customer's data.

62
00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:14.638
The housekeeping is remove, in a finally block, so the thread goes back clean.

63
00:04:14.638 --> 00:04:19.884
On JDK 25, scoped values, which last for one call only, clean up for you.

64
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.906
Some state is shared and changing by its nature, like a bank balance.

65
00:04:24.906 --> 00:04:28.564
That still needs the locks and atomics from earlier lessons.

66
00:04:29.367 --> 00:04:33.647
Here's the board swap in code, Java on top and Kotlin below.

67
00:04:33.647 --> 00:04:34.859
A volatile field.

68
00:04:34.859 --> 00:04:38.711
Build an immutable copy, then publish it in one write.

69
00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:40.209
Readers take no lock.

70
00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:43.504
So, can threads read with no key at all?

71
00:04:43.504 --> 00:04:45.913
Yes, when nothing they read can change.

72
00:04:45.913 --> 00:04:47.889
A race needs sharing and change.

73
00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:52.089
Before you reach for a lock, ask which ingredient you can take away.

74
00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.044
Quick check.

75
00:04:53.044 --> 00:04:57.695
A waiter puts a ticket on the rail, then clears it to write the next order.

76
00:04:57.695 --> 00:04:59.680
Which ingredient just came back?
